@@ -0,0 +1,48 @@
+# smallpt browser port
+
+Original: https://www.kevinbeason.com/smallpt/ by Kevin Beason, MIT license.
+`smallpt-original.cpp` is the unmodified upstream source. `smallpt.cpp` retains
+its scene, intersection and recursive radiance implementation, replacing the
+command-line entry point with incremental rendering and two sphere-color setters.
+The upstream license is kept here and shipped alongside the WebAssembly assets.
+
+## Build
+
+Requires Emscripten (`em++`) and the project's Node/pnpm dependencies:
+
+```sh
+pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
+pnpm run build
+```
+
+`build` first compiles `public/wasm/smallpt.js` and `smallpt.wasm`, then builds
+Astro. `pnpm run build:wasm` rebuilds only the renderer. The homepage is
+prerendered to `dist/client/index.html`; assets respect Astro's configured base
+path (`/devtools-times/`). The checked-in Wasm artifacts allow the page to run
+without a compiler at runtime.
+
+## Runtime
+
+A dedicated module Worker loads the Emscripten ES module and traces short row
+batches, yielding between them. Pixel buffers transfer back to the main thread
+for a Canvas 2D display. The tracer accumulates independent samples in each of
+four tent-filtered subpixels. Quality selects total samples per pixel (spp).
+Changing colors, quality or resolution starts a fresh render; newer jobs cancel
+older jobs. No render service or cross-origin isolation headers are required.
+
+`public/renders/cornell.png` is a startup preview produced by this same native
+tracer at 640 × 480, 128 spp. It remains visible until the first full Wasm pass.
+The browser automatically starts a live render on load.
+
+The native entry point can also produce raw RGBA for offline comparison:
+
+```sh
+g++ -O3 native/smallpt.cpp -o /tmp/smallpt-native
+/tmp/smallpt-native 640 128
+# Writes /tmp/smallpt-preview.rgba (640 × 480, RGBA bytes)
+```
+
+Verification performed: production build; non-browser execution of the shipped
+Wasm module checking completion, heap growth, deterministic seeds, opaque visible
+pixels, and changes to output when sphere colors change. No development/preview
+server or browser was used.
